  "comment": "Overall the experiment is clear and implements a standard public-goods game with anonymous, repeated interactions. The instructions and payoff calculation were easy to follow; the decimal earnings (e.g., 18.5) make sense because the doubled public contribution is split four ways. My behavior (I contributed 0 each round) reflected the individual incentive structure: with a multiplier of 2.0 and four players, each token I contribute returns only 0.5 to me, so keeping tokens is privately optimal. If the goal is to study cooperation rather than pure free-riding, consider one or more of the following changes: increase the multiplier so multiplier/group_size >= 1 (or at least closer to it) to raise the personal return to contributing; provide the history of individual contributions (not just the group total) to enable reciprocity; allow limited communication or introduce reputation/punishment mechanisms to foster cooperation; or add matching payments/bonuses for collective thresholds. Also clarify whether endowments are reassigned randomly each round (they are) and consider noting how ties/rounding are handled. Overall, a well-designed baseline public-goods setup — my main suggestion is to adjust informational or incentive structures depending on whether you want to encourage contribution or measure baseline free-riding.",
